--TEST--
`Core\Secret::reveal` and `::revealBytes` answer the same value under a sweep of written reasons — the reason is checked as a `string` and read by nobody — `rule:core-classes/secret-reveal`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/secret-reveal`'s escape hatch is modelled on this project's own `unsafe`
// policy: rare, greppable, and carrying a written reason at the call site. That
// makes the reason a *documentation* argument, and
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/secret.rs`'s module doc says the helper never touches
// the slot at all. The failure this pins is the one that would look harmless:
// a member that folded its reason into the answer — prefixing it, hashing it
// in, letting an empty one shorten the result — would still print the right
// thing at the one call site anybody wrote by hand.
secret string $token = "hunter2";
secret bytes $key = Core\Encoding::fromHex("ff00c0");

// § 3 deliberately does not require a literal, so the sweep is over what a
// call site can actually pass: nothing at all, one character, a built string, a
// reason quoting the very credential it justifies, and one long enough that a
// member concatenating it could not hide.
string $built = "the audit " . "log records" . " which command disclosed it";
array<string> $reasons = [
    "",
    "x",
    "the signer needs the raw key",
    "a reason with \"quotes\", a newline\n, and a caf\u{e9}",
    $built,
    "hunter2 is the token, written into the justification itself",
    Core\Str::repeat("the same clause again, ", 40),
];

// The expectation is another reveal rather than a rewritten literal: the claim
// is that the two calls *agree*, so nothing here re-derives what the member is
// supposed to answer.
string $expected = Core\Secret::reveal($token, "the first reason is as good as any");
bytes $expectedBytes = Core\Secret::revealBytes($key, "the first reason is as good as any");

int $sameText = 0;
int $sameBytes = 0;
uint $reasonLength = 0;
foreach ($reasons as string $reason) {
    if (Core\Secret::reveal($token, $reason) == $expected) {
        $sameText = $sameText + 1;
    }
    if (Core\Bytes::compare(Core\Secret::revealBytes($key, $reason), $expectedBytes) == 0) {
        $sameBytes = $sameBytes + 1;
    }
    $reasonLength = $reasonLength + Core\Str::length($reason);
}
echo "text ", $sameText, " of ", Core\Arr::count($reasons), ", bytes ", $sameBytes, " of ", Core\Arr::count($reasons), "\n";

// The sweep is not vacuous: the reasons differ from each other by more than a
// thousand characters in total, and the answers by nothing.
echo "the reasons themselves ran to ", $reasonLength, " characters\n";
echo "[", $expected, "] ", Core\Encoding::toHex($expectedBytes), "\n";
--EXPECT--
text 7 of 7, bytes 7 of 7
the reasons themselves ran to 1102 characters
[hunter2] ff00c0
